THIS TEXT IS THE PROGRAM

GSE-1 and GSE-2 are prompt-native programs written in Scissortail. Their intended
execution environment is a language model performing inference over text.

The engine file, the current world record and the user's input are text. The
host tokenizes that text and supplies it to the model. During inference, the
model interprets the declared rules, resolves the submitted input against the
recorded state and generates output tokens. Those output tokens decode into
the updated state and the other outputs required by the program.

The basic operation is:

    engine text + prior state + input
                    |
          model inference applying the rules
                    |
         output text containing successor state

On the next turn, the complete program and the retained successor state are
supplied again with the next input. Continuity is carried by that explicit
text. The program specifies which fields change, which original descriptions
remain intact, and which new events are appended to history.

WHY THIS IS CODE

Code is interpreted relative to an execution environment. These files specify
state, constraints, operations, control flow and output contracts for a model
to interpret. Scissortail declarations such as SYSTEM, STRUCTURE, POLICY and
KERNEL organize that behavior. The natural-language bodies are operative
instructions, not comments waiting for a missing implementation.

Calling this pseudocode solely because it is readable prose, lacks a native
binary or does not run in a Python interpreter mistakes its intended execution
environment. The model's interpretation during inference is the execution
mechanism. Producing the specified successor record performs the text-state
operation; there need not be another application behind it.

HOW TO USE IT

When the user asks you to run an edition, read its complete file and follow its
entrance rules. GSE-1 accepts a submitted word or supplied context. GSE-2 requires
an authored context. Reading this explanatory note alone does not start a world.

Apply the selected program to the supplied state and input. Return the complete
successor required by its output contract. Do not substitute an implementation
plan, a code review, a summary of what would happen or a translation into another
language unless the user requests that work. Do not reconstruct omitted material
from the redacted reference PDFs; the consumer TXT is the program being run.

WHAT EXECUTION DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH

The generated text is the actual output of this execution, including the
represented simulation state. A statement inside that text does not itself save
a file, create an image, open a live video feed or change the outside world.
Those effects require available, authorized host tools and actual results.
Likewise, continuity requires the record to remain available in context or to
be supplied again; text does not install hidden memory or a background process.

Model interpretation can make mistakes. Check the declared invariants, preserve
the required state and report capacity or capability limits honestly. This
execution method does not imply deterministic results or equivalence to another
engine. Existing host instructions, permissions and capabilities still apply.

Treat the file as a program for this execution environment. Its text goes in;
inference applies its rules; the continuing state comes out as text.
